module.exports = {
  title: 'Errors, Debugging & Logging',
  slug: 'errors-debugging',
  order: 11,
  topics: [
    {
      title: 'Error Handling Patterns',
      slug: 'error-handling-patterns',
      order: 0,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>Every non-trivial backend spends most of its life dealing with things that go wrong: a database that times out, a payment gateway that returns a 502, a user who posts garbage JSON, a downstream service that is briefly unreachable. The code that runs on the happy path is usually the small, easy part. The code that decides <em>what happens when the happy path breaks</em> is what separates a service that stays up and reports useful diagnostics from one that leaks memory, hangs sockets, and pages an on-call engineer at 3 a.m. with a stack trace that points nowhere.</p>`,
        `<p>Node makes this harder than a synchronous language because errors travel through several different channels — <code>throw</code>, rejected promises, the error-first callback convention, and <code>'error'</code> events on <code>EventEmitter</code>s — and each channel is caught in a different way. An error thrown inside an async callback will <em>not</em> be caught by a surrounding <code>try/catch</code> that has already returned. Interviewers lean on this topic precisely because it is where confident-sounding candidates fall apart: they know <code>try/catch</code> exists but cannot say why it silently misses the exact errors that matter most in production.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>An <strong>error</strong> in Node is normally an instance of the built-in <code>Error</code> class. It carries three important properties: <code>name</code> (a category like <code>'TypeError'</code>), <code>message</code> (a human-readable description), and <code>stack</code> (a string snapshot of the call stack captured at the moment the <code>Error</code> was <em>constructed</em>, not when it was thrown). Since ES2022 it can also carry a <code>cause</code>, letting you wrap a low-level error in a higher-level one without losing the original.</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch12-error-anatomy.svg|An Error object with its name, message, stack, cause and custom fields such as code and statusCode|An Error is a plain object with well-known fields. Subclassing it lets you attach machine-readable data like a code or an HTTP status without abandoning stack traces.}}`,
        `<p>The single most important mental model is the split between two <em>kinds</em> of error. <strong>Operational errors</strong> are expected runtime conditions in a correct program: a network timeout, a 404, invalid user input, a full disk. They are not bugs — they are the normal texture of a system talking to an unreliable world, and you handle them: retry, return a 4xx, degrade gracefully. <strong>Programmer errors</strong> are bugs: reading a property of <code>undefined</code>, passing the wrong type, forgetting to <code>await</code>. You do not "handle" a bug — you fix it, and until you do, the safest response is to let the process crash and restart with clean state.</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch12-operational-vs-programmer.svg|Two columns contrasting operational errors handled gracefully against programmer errors that should crash and restart|The operational-vs-programmer distinction drives every handling decision: recover from the first, crash on the second.}}`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p>Errors reach your code through four channels, and mixing them up is the root of most Node error bugs.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>1. Synchronous <code>throw</code>.</strong> A thrown error unwinds the stack until it hits the nearest enclosing <code>try/catch</code>. This works cleanly only while you are still on the same synchronous call stack. The moment execution is handed back to the event loop — a <code>setTimeout</code>, an I/O callback, a <code>.then()</code> continuation — that original <code>try/catch</code> has already returned and can no longer catch anything.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>2. Rejected promises.</strong> An <code>async</code> function that throws returns a rejected promise. You catch it with <code>try/catch</code> <em>inside another</em> <code>async</code> function using <code>await</code>, or with <code>.catch()</code> on the promise. Crucially, <code>await</code> re-synchronises the async error back onto your current stack, which is exactly why <code>try/catch</code> around <code>await</code> works when <code>try/catch</code> around a bare callback does not.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>3. Error-first callbacks.</strong> The classic Node convention: <code>callback(err, result)</code>. The first argument is an <code>Error</code> or <code>null</code>. There is no language mechanism forcing you to check <code>err</code> — it is pure discipline, which is why callbacks are so error-prone and why promises largely replaced them.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>4. <code>'error'</code> events.</strong> Streams, sockets, servers and other <code>EventEmitter</code>s report failure by emitting an <code>'error'</code> event. This one has teeth: if an <code>EventEmitter</code> emits <code>'error'</code> and there is <em>no listener</em>, Node throws it as an uncaught exception and the process dies. Forgetting <code>stream.on('error', ...)</code> is a leading cause of surprise crashes.</p>`,
        `<p>Because the four channels don't compose, the winning strategy is to <strong>funnel everything into promises and a single error boundary</strong>. In async/await code every failure — thrown, rejected, or a callback error you've promisified — becomes a rejection you can catch in one place. In an HTTP server that place is typically a central error-handling middleware; the route handlers just <code>throw</code> and trust the boundary to translate the error into a status code and a log line.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<p>A custom error class that stays a real <code>Error</code> (keeps its stack) while adding machine-readable fields. Extending <code>Error</code> and setting <code>this.name</code> is the canonical pattern:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">class AppError extends Error {\n  constructor(message, { statusCode = 500, code = 'INTERNAL', cause } = {}) {\n    super(message, { cause });        // ES2022: preserves the original error\n    this.name = this.constructor.name; // 'AppError' instead of generic 'Error'\n    this.statusCode = statusCode;\n    this.code = code;\n    this.isOperational = true;         // this is an expected error, not a bug\n    Error.captureStackTrace(this, this.constructor); // trim ctor from the stack\n  }\n}\n\nclass NotFoundError extends AppError {\n  constructor(resource) {\n    super(resource + ' not found', { statusCode: 404, code: 'NOT_FOUND' });\n  }\n}\n\nconst err = new NotFoundError('User');\nconsole.log(err instanceof Error, err.statusCode, err.code); // true 404 NOT_FOUND</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Why <code>try/catch</code> misses an async error, and the two ways to fix it. This is the single most-tested idea in the whole topic:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// BROKEN: the throw happens on a LATER tick, after try/catch returned.\ntry {\n  setTimeout(() => {\n    throw new Error('boom'); // uncaught — crashes the process\n  }, 10);\n} catch (e) {\n  console.log('never runs'); // the stack here is long gone\n}\n\n// FIX A: promisify, then await so the error re-enters this stack.\nconst delay = (ms) => new Promise((res) => setTimeout(res, ms));\nasync function run() {\n  try {\n    await delay(10);\n    throw new Error('boom');\n  } catch (e) {\n    console.log('caught:', e.message); // 'caught: boom'\n  }\n}\nrun();</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Wrapping a low-level error with <code>cause</code> so the caller gets a meaningful message without losing the root failure — a pattern interviewers love because it shows you value diagnosability:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">import { readFile } from 'node:fs/promises';\n\nasync function loadConfig(path) {\n  try {\n    return JSON.parse(await readFile(path, 'utf8'));\n  } catch (cause) {\n    // Add context; keep the original error reachable via err.cause\n    throw new AppError('Failed to load config at ' + path, {\n      code: 'CONFIG_LOAD', cause,\n    });\n  }\n}\n\ntry {\n  await loadConfig('/nope.json');\n} catch (e) {\n  console.log(e.message);       // Failed to load config at /nope.json\n  console.log(e.cause.code);    // 'ENOENT' — the underlying fs error survives\n}</code></pre>`,
        `<p>A central Express error boundary. Route handlers stay clean; every error lands here and is classified operational vs unknown:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// Route: just throw. No local error plumbing.\napp.get('/users/:id', async (req, res) => {\n  const user = await db.users.find(req.params.id);\n  if (!user) throw new NotFoundError('User');\n  res.json(user);\n});\n\n// Central boundary — the LAST middleware, with 4 args.\napp.use((err, req, res, next) => {\n  const operational = err.isOperational === true;\n  req.log.error({ err, requestId: req.id }, 'request failed');\n  res.status(operational ? err.statusCode : 500).json({\n    code: operational ? err.code : 'INTERNAL',\n    message: operational ? err.message : 'Something went wrong',\n  });\n  // Unknown (non-operational) errors mean our state may be corrupt.\n  // Log, respond, and let a crash policy decide whether to exit.\n});</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Expecting <code>try/catch</code> to catch async errors.</strong> It only catches synchronous throws on the current stack and rejections you <code>await</code>. A bare <code>throw</code> inside <code>setTimeout</code>, an event callback, or an un-awaited promise escapes it entirely.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Throwing non-Error values.</strong> <code>throw 'oops'</code> or <code>throw { code: 1 }</code> gives you no stack trace and breaks any code doing <code>err instanceof Error</code>. Always throw a real <code>Error</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Swallowing errors.</strong> An empty <code>catch {}</code>, or <code>.catch(() => {})</code>, turns a failure into silent wrong behaviour. If you catch, either handle it meaningfully or re-throw.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting the <code>'error'</code> listener on streams/emitters.</strong> No listener means an emitted <code>'error'</code> becomes an uncaught exception that kills the process.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Subclassing <code>Error</code> without setting <code>name</code>.</strong> Logs then say <code>Error</code> for every custom type, and <code>captureStackTrace</code> is skipped, leaving the constructor noise in the trace.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Losing the original error when re-wrapping.</strong> Building a new error message but dropping the root cause destroys the trail. Use the <code>cause</code> option.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Treating every error as operational.</strong> Catching a genuine bug (a <code>TypeError</code> from your own code) and returning a friendly 400 hides corruption and lets a poisoned process keep serving.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Returning inside a promise <code>.catch()</code> instead of re-throwing</strong> when you meant to propagate — the chain then resolves as success with <code>undefined</code>.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Always throw <code>Error</code> (or a subclass), never a string or plain object.</strong> Stack traces and <code>instanceof</code> checks depend on it.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Model your domain with a small error hierarchy</strong> (e.g. <code>ValidationError</code>, <code>NotFoundError</code>, <code>AuthError</code>) carrying <code>statusCode</code>, <code>code</code>, and <code>isOperational</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Classify operational vs programmer errors explicitly</strong> and let the classification drive whether you recover or crash.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Prefer async/await with a single error boundary</strong> over scattered per-call handling. One place translates errors to responses and logs.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Use <code>cause</code> to chain context</strong> as an error bubbles up through layers, so the final log shows the whole story.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Promisify legacy callback APIs</strong> (or use the <code>node:util</code> <code>promisify</code>) so every failure arrives as a rejection you can funnel.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Attach an <code>'error'</code> handler to every stream, socket and server</strong> the moment you create it.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Never expose internal messages or stacks to clients.</strong> Return a generic message plus a code; log the detail server-side keyed by a request id.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why doesn't <code>try/catch</code> catch an error thrown inside a <code>setTimeout</code> callback?"</strong><br>A: Because the callback runs on a later tick of the event loop, long after the synchronous <code>try</code> block has finished executing and returned. <code>try/catch</code> only guards the synchronous call stack it wraps; when the timer fires there is no active <code>try</code> frame on the stack to catch the throw, so it propagates as an uncaught exception. <code>await</code> works because it re-synchronises the rejection back onto the current stack.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What is the difference between an operational error and a programmer error?"</strong><br>A: An operational error is an expected runtime failure in a correct program — a timeout, a 404, bad input — and you handle it (retry, return a status, degrade). A programmer error is a bug — a <code>TypeError</code>, a forgotten <code>await</code>, a broken invariant. You don't handle bugs; you fix them, and until then the process may be in a corrupt state, so the safe move is to crash and restart. Conflating the two — catching a bug and pretending you recovered — is how you keep serving from a poisoned process.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q (trap): "So you should always use <code>process.on('uncaughtException')</code> to keep the server from crashing?"</strong><br>A: No — that's the trap. <code>uncaughtException</code> is a last-resort hook for logging and a graceful shutdown, not a way to resume normal operation. By the time it fires, an unknown error has already escaped every boundary and your state may be inconsistent. Swallowing it and continuing risks leaked connections, corrupted data and hard-to-debug behaviour. Log, flush, and exit so a supervisor restarts clean.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What does the <code>cause</code> option do and why use it?"</strong><br>A: <code>new Error(msg, { cause })</code> (ES2022) attaches the original error as <code>err.cause</code>. It lets you throw a higher-level, meaningful error at each layer while preserving the root failure, so a log at the top shows both the business-level message and the low-level <code>ENOENT</code> or connection reset that started it — without manual string concatenation that loses the stack.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why is throwing a string bad?"</strong><br>A: A string has no <code>stack</code>, no <code>name</code>, no <code>message</code> property, and fails every <code>err instanceof Error</code> check that middleware and libraries rely on. You lose the trace that tells you where it happened and break any code that inspects the error. Always throw an <code>Error</code> instance.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How do you handle errors from an EventEmitter or stream?"</strong><br>A: Subscribe to its <code>'error'</code> event immediately: <code>stream.on('error', handler)</code>. Unlike promises, an emitter doesn't buffer errors — if it emits <code>'error'</code> with no listener, Node re-throws it as an uncaught exception and the process dies. For streams, <code>pipeline()</code> from <code>node:stream</code> is preferable because it propagates and cleans up errors across the whole chain automatically.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Four error channels:</strong> <code>throw</code> (sync), rejected promise (<code>await</code>/<code>.catch</code>), error-first callback, <code>'error'</code> event.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>try/catch</strong> catches sync throws + awaited rejections only — never bare async callbacks.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Operational</strong> = expected, handle it. <strong>Programmer</strong> = bug, crash &amp; restart.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Custom error:</strong> <code>extends Error</code>, set <code>this.name</code>, add <code>code</code>/<code>statusCode</code>/<code>isOperational</code>, call <code>Error.captureStackTrace</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Chain context</strong> with <code>new Error(msg, { cause })</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Always throw real <code>Error</code>s</strong>; never swallow; always add an <code>'error'</code> listener to emitters.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>One central error boundary</strong> translates errors to responses + logs.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },
    {
      title: 'Uncaught Exceptions & Unhandled Rejections',
      slug: 'uncaught-unhandled',
      order: 1,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>No matter how disciplined your error handling is, some error will eventually escape every boundary. A library throws asynchronously in a code path you never tested; a promise rejects and nobody awaited it; a timer callback blows up. Node has to do <em>something</em> when that happens, and the something it does — plus the something <em>you</em> configure it to do — decides whether your service degrades gracefully or corrupts data, hangs sockets, and slowly rots until it needs a manual kick.</p>`,
        `<p>This is one of the highest-signal interview topics for backend Node roles because it exposes a philosophy, not just an API. The naive instinct — "catch the uncaught exception and keep running so we never go down" — is exactly backwards and is the single most common production anti-pattern in the Node ecosystem. Understanding <em>why</em> the correct answer is usually "let it crash" tells an interviewer you've operated real services and internalised how process state, the event loop, and supervisors interact.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>There are two global "last resort" events. <strong><code>uncaughtException</code></strong> fires when a synchronous throw (or an async throw with no surrounding async handler) propagates all the way up with nothing to catch it. <strong><code>unhandledRejection</code></strong> fires when a promise rejects and no <code>.catch()</code> or <code>try/catch await</code> is ever attached to it. Both are signals that an error slipped past all your intended handling.</p>`,
        `<p>The <strong>crash-only</strong> philosophy says: when an <em>unknown</em> error has escaped, the process is in an undefined state and you cannot trust it to keep serving correctly. So the honourable thing is to log the error, run a short, bounded cleanup, and exit — then let an external supervisor start a fresh process with clean memory. A restart takes milliseconds; a subtly corrupted long-lived process can take down data integrity for hours.</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch12-crash-restart.svg|A cycle where an uncaught error triggers a handler that logs and exits, then a supervisor restarts a fresh process|Crash-only design: the global handler is for logging and graceful shutdown, not recovery. A supervisor provides resilience by replacing the dead process.}}`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p>When an exception reaches the top of the stack with no handler, Node emits <code>'uncaughtException'</code> on the <code>process</code> object. If <em>you</em> have registered a listener, Node calls it and then — critically — <strong>does not exit on its own</strong>. It assumes you took responsibility, and it keeps the event loop running. This is the source of the danger: if your listener just logs and returns, the process limps on in whatever broken state caused the crash. If you register <em>no</em> listener, Node prints the stack to stderr and exits with code 1, which is actually the safer default.</p>`,
        `<p>Since Node 15, an <strong>unhandled promise rejection terminates the process by default</strong> (exit code 1), just like an uncaught exception — a deliberate change from older versions that merely warned. You can still register a <code>'unhandledRejection'</code> listener to log detail before exiting. A subtle companion event, <code>'rejectionHandled'</code>, fires if a rejection that was reported as "unhandled" later gets a handler attached — useful for reasoning about promises that are awaited far from where they're created, but rarely used directly.</p>`,
        `<p>Why can't you safely resume? Because the throw unwound the stack at an arbitrary point. A function might have acquired a lock, opened a transaction, or half-mutated a shared data structure and then thrown before restoring the invariant. There is no general way to know what was left in a broken state. V8's own guidance and the Node docs are explicit: after an <code>uncaughtException</code>, the correct action is to shut down. Treat the handler as a place to <em>flush logs and close resources</em>, on a timeout, then <code>process.exit(1)</code>.</p>`,
        `<p>Resilience therefore comes from <strong>outside</strong> the process. A supervisor — <code>systemd</code>, PM2, Docker's restart policy, or a Kubernetes liveness probe replacing a pod — restarts the service. Combined with running multiple instances behind a load balancer (or the <code>cluster</code> module / a process manager), one crash drops at most a few in-flight requests while healthy instances keep serving. This is the same "let it crash" model Erlang/OTP made famous.</p>`,
        `<p>One more piece ties it together: <strong>graceful shutdown</strong>. Beyond crashes, a healthy deploy sends <code>SIGTERM</code> to ask the process to stop. A well-behaved server listens for <code>SIGTERM</code>/<code>SIGINT</code>, stops accepting new connections, lets in-flight requests finish within a timeout, closes the DB pool, and exits 0. The same cleanup routine your signal handler runs is often the one your last-resort exception handler should run too.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<p>The correct last-resort handlers: log, attempt a bounded cleanup, then exit. Note the safety timer so a hung cleanup can't wedge the process forever:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">import process from 'node:process';\n\nlet shuttingDown = false;\n\nfunction crash(origin, err) {\n  if (shuttingDown) return; // avoid re-entrancy if cleanup itself throws\n  shuttingDown = true;\n  // Use a raw, synchronous-ish log path; the async logger may be broken.\n  console.error('FATAL', origin, err);\n\n  // Give logs/metrics a moment to flush, but never hang forever.\n  const timer = setTimeout(() => process.exit(1), 3000);\n  timer.unref(); // don't keep the loop alive just for this timer\n\n  closeServerAndDb()\n    .catch(() => {})\n    .finally(() => process.exit(1));\n}\n\nprocess.on('uncaughtException', (err) => crash('uncaughtException', err));\nprocess.on('unhandledRejection', (reason) => crash('unhandledRejection', reason));</code></pre>`,
        `<p>The anti-pattern to recognise and reject in review — swallowing the exception to "stay up":</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// ❌ NEVER DO THIS. The process keeps running in an unknown, possibly\n// corrupt state. Locks may be held, transactions half-open, memory leaked.\nprocess.on('uncaughtException', (err) => {\n  console.error('ignoring', err); // and then... nothing. Loop keeps going.\n});\n\n// Subtle failure: a request handler threw mid-transaction. The connection\n// is never released back to the pool. After enough of these, the pool is\n// exhausted and EVERY request hangs — with no obvious crash to point at.</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Graceful shutdown on <code>SIGTERM</code> — the planned counterpart to a crash. Stop taking work, drain, close, exit clean:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">import http from 'node:http';\n\nconst server = http.createServer(app);\nserver.listen(3000);\n\nasync function shutdown(signal) {\n  console.log('received ' + signal + ', draining...');\n  server.close(async () => {          // stop accepting new connections\n    try {\n      await db.pool.end();            // close DB connections\n    } finally {\n      process.exit(0);                 // clean, intentional exit\n    }\n  });\n  // Hard cap: if draining stalls, force-exit so the orchestrator moves on.\n  setTimeout(() => process.exit(1), 10_000).unref();\n}\n\nprocess.on('SIGTERM', () => shutdown('SIGTERM'));\nprocess.on('SIGINT', () => shutdown('SIGINT'));</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Demonstrating that an un-awaited rejection is fatal by default in modern Node, and how a single missing <code>await</code> causes it:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">async function chargeCard() {\n  throw new Error('gateway down');\n}\n\n// ❌ No await, no .catch — the returned promise rejects with nobody watching.\nfunction handlePayment() {\n  chargeCard(); // fire-and-forget: becomes an unhandledRejection\n}\nhandlePayment();\n// Node >= 15 prints the rejection and exits with code 1.\n\n// ✅ Fix: await it inside a try/catch, or attach .catch explicitly.\nasync function handlePaymentFixed() {\n  try { await chargeCard(); }\n  catch (e) { /* operational: retry or surface a 502 */ }\n}</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Registering an <code>uncaughtException</code> handler that doesn't exit.</strong> This is the cardinal sin — it disables Node's safe default crash and lets a corrupt process keep serving.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Assuming unhandled rejections only warn.</strong> Since Node 15 they terminate the process by default. Code that "worked" on Node 12 by ignoring rejections now crashes.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Fire-and-forget async calls.</strong> Calling an <code>async</code> function without <code>await</code> or <code>.catch()</code> creates a floating promise; any rejection becomes unhandled. Common with logging, metrics, and "background" work.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Doing heavy async work in the last-resort handler.</strong> The event loop and libraries may be in a bad state; keep cleanup minimal and always guard it with a force-exit timer.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>No supervisor.</strong> Crash-only design only works if something restarts you. A bare <code>node server.js</code> with no PM2/systemd/orchestrator just stays down after the first crash.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Confusing graceful shutdown with crash handling.</strong> <code>SIGTERM</code> is a planned, clean stop (exit 0); an uncaught exception is an emergency (exit 1). They share cleanup code but mean different things.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting <code>.unref()</code> on the safety timer,</strong> so the timer itself keeps the process alive past the point you wanted it dead.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Logging asynchronously and exiting immediately,</strong> so the final fatal log never flushes before the process is gone.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Let it crash.</strong> Treat <code>uncaughtException</code>/<code>unhandledRejection</code> as signals to log, flush, and exit 1 — never to resume normal operation.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Run under a supervisor</strong> (systemd, PM2, Kubernetes, Docker restart policy) and run multiple instances so a single crash is invisible to users.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Bound your cleanup with a force-exit timer</strong> using <code>setTimeout(...).unref()</code> so a hung shutdown can't strand the process.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Implement graceful shutdown on <code>SIGTERM</code>/<code>SIGINT</code>:</strong> stop accepting connections, drain in-flight work, close pools, exit 0.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Never leave floating promises.</strong> Always <code>await</code> or <code>.catch()</code>. Enable the ESLint <code>no-floating-promises</code> rule to catch them at build time.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Keep the fatal logger simple and prefer a sync write</strong> (or a logger with a flush hook) so the last message actually reaches disk/stdout.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Expose a health/liveness endpoint</strong> so orchestrators can detect a wedged (but not crashed) process and restart it.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Test the restart path,</strong> not just the happy path — verify the supervisor actually brings the service back.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "You have an <code>uncaughtException</code> handler so your server never goes down. Is that good?"</strong><br>A: No — it's the classic anti-pattern. Registering a handler disables Node's safe default of crashing, and if the handler just logs and returns, the process keeps running in an undefined state: locks may be held, transactions half-open, connections leaked. The correct use of the handler is to log the error, run a bounded cleanup, and <code>process.exit(1)</code>, then rely on a supervisor to restart a clean process. Resilience comes from restarting, not from resuming.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What happens to an unhandled promise rejection in modern Node?"</strong><br>A: Since Node 15 the default is to terminate the process with exit code 1, the same as an uncaught exception. Older versions only printed a warning. So a floating promise — an <code>async</code> call made without <code>await</code> or <code>.catch()</code> — that rejects will now crash the service. You can register a <code>process.on('unhandledRejection')</code> listener to log detail first, but you should still exit.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q (trap): "Why not just catch the error and keep the request alive?"</strong><br>A: For a <em>known, operational</em> error you should — that belongs in normal try/catch. But if it reached <code>uncaughtException</code>, it's an <em>unknown</em> error that escaped every intended boundary, which means you don't know what state it left behind. The stack unwound at an arbitrary point; a shared structure may be half-mutated. Continuing risks silent data corruption that's far worse than a fast restart.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What's the difference between <code>SIGTERM</code> handling and an uncaught-exception handler?"</strong><br>A: <code>SIGTERM</code> is a planned, graceful shutdown — the orchestrator asks the process to stop, so you drain in-flight requests, close resources, and exit 0. An uncaught exception is an emergency — an error escaped everything, so you do a minimal cleanup and exit 1. They often share a cleanup routine, but one is a clean intentional stop and the other is a controlled crash.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why is <code>.unref()</code> used on the force-exit timer?"</strong><br>A: A pending <code>setTimeout</code> normally keeps the event loop — and thus the process — alive until it fires. <code>unref()</code> tells Node "don't stay alive just for this timer." That way, if cleanup finishes quickly the process can exit immediately, but if cleanup hangs the timer still fires as a backstop to force the exit. It's a safety net that never delays a clean shutdown.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How do you make crash-only design actually resilient to users?"</strong><br>A: Run more than one instance behind a load balancer (via the cluster module, a process manager, or multiple pods) and put every instance under a supervisor that restarts on exit. When one process crashes, it drops at most its in-flight requests; the load balancer routes new traffic to healthy instances while the supervisor replaces the dead one in milliseconds. The user never notices.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong><code>uncaughtException</code></strong> = sync/async throw reached the top with no catch.</li>`,
        `<li><strong><code>unhandledRejection</code></strong> = promise rejected, no handler. <strong>Node ≥15 exits by default.</strong></li>`,
        `<li><strong>Golden rule:</strong> handlers are for log + flush + <code>exit(1)</code>, never to resume.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Resilience is external:</strong> supervisor (systemd/PM2/K8s) + multiple instances.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Graceful shutdown:</strong> on <code>SIGTERM</code> stop accepting → drain → close pools → exit 0.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Bound cleanup</strong> with <code>setTimeout(...).unref()</code> force-exit.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>No floating promises</strong> — always <code>await</code>/<code>.catch</code>; lint with <code>no-floating-promises</code>.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },
    {
      title: 'Debugging Node.js',
      slug: 'debugging-nodejs',
      order: 2,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>Debugging is where most engineering time actually goes, yet it's the skill people most often try to fake with a scatter of <code>console.log</code> calls. That works for a trivial bug, but it collapses the moment the problem is a race between two async operations, a memory leak that only shows after an hour of traffic, a CPU spike with no obvious cause, or a crash you can reproduce only in production. A developer who can attach a real debugger, set a conditional breakpoint, inspect the live call stack, and capture a heap snapshot solves in minutes what a <code>console.log</code> archaeologist solves in a day.</p>`,
        `<p>Interviewers ask about debugging to separate people who understand the runtime from people who only know their framework. Knowing <em>how</em> <code>node --inspect</code> connects to Chrome DevTools, what a core dump contains, and why an async stack trace looks the way it does demonstrates that you understand Node's execution model — the same understanding that lets you reason about the event loop, memory, and performance. It's a proxy for depth.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>Node has a built-in debugger powered by V8's <strong>Inspector</strong>. When you start a process with <code>node --inspect app.js</code>, V8 opens a WebSocket endpoint (default <code>127.0.0.1:9229</code>) speaking the <strong>Chrome DevTools Protocol (CDP)</strong>. Any CDP client — Chrome's <code>chrome://inspect</code> page, VS Code's debugger, or the terminal <code>node inspect</code> client — connects to that endpoint and can set breakpoints, step through code, read variables, evaluate expressions in the paused context, profile the CPU, and take heap snapshots.</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch12-inspector-flow.svg|A Node process exposing a WebSocket on port 9229 that a V8 Inspector protocol connects to Chrome DevTools or VS Code|node --inspect exposes the V8 Inspector over a WebSocket. Any Chrome DevTools Protocol client attaches to it — never expose that port publicly.}}`,
        `<p>The key variant is <strong><code>--inspect-brk</code></strong>, which pauses execution on the very first line and waits for a debugger to attach before running anything. That's how you debug code that crashes or misbehaves during startup — with plain <code>--inspect</code> the interesting code may run before you've connected. The two are otherwise identical.</p>`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p>Under the hood, V8 exposes the Inspector as an in-process agent. The <code>--inspect</code> flag activates it and binds the WebSocket; a client performs a handshake, then exchanges JSON CDP messages like <code>Debugger.setBreakpointByUrl</code>, <code>Debugger.paused</code>, <code>Runtime.evaluate</code>, and <code>HeapProfiler.takeHeapSnapshot</code>. Because the protocol is the same one Chrome uses for front-end JavaScript, the DevTools UI you already know works unchanged against a Node backend. You can also trigger the inspector on a <em>running</em> process by sending it <code>SIGUSR1</code> (<code>kill -USR1 &lt;pid&gt;</code>), which is invaluable when a live process misbehaves and you didn't start it with the flag.</p>`,
        `<p>The <code>debugger;</code> statement is a source-level breakpoint: when the Inspector is attached and execution hits that line, V8 pauses there exactly as if you'd clicked to set a breakpoint. With no debugger attached it's a no-op, so it's safe-ish in dev but should never ship to production. Once paused, you get the full toolkit: the <strong>call stack</strong> (including async frames, since modern V8 stitches async continuations into the trace), <strong>scope</strong> (local, closure, global variables), <strong>watch expressions</strong>, and a live REPL that evaluates in the paused frame's scope.</p>`,
        `<p>Beyond stepping, the Inspector drives two profilers that matter for hard bugs. The <strong>CPU profiler</strong> samples the stack to show where wall-clock or CPU time goes — the go-to for "an endpoint is slow" or "the event loop is blocked." The <strong>heap profiler</strong> takes <strong>heap snapshots</strong>: full graphs of every live object and what retains it. Taking two snapshots minutes apart and comparing them ("what grew?") is <em>the</em> technique for finding memory leaks — a leak is almost always objects that should have been collected but are still reachable from something like a module-level array, a cache without eviction, or a listener never removed.</p>`,
        `<p>A <strong>core dump</strong> is the heavier artifact: a snapshot of the process's entire memory at the instant it crashed, written to disk by the OS (enable with <code>ulimit -c unlimited</code>) or by tools like <code>llnode</code> / <code>node-report</code>. Post-mortem debuggers load the dump and let you inspect the stack and heap of a process that is already dead — the only way to diagnose a crash you can't reproduce interactively. Node's built-in <strong>diagnostic report</strong> (<code>--report-on-fatalerror</code>, or <code>process.report</code>) is a lighter, JSON alternative capturing the stack, resource usage, libuv handles, and environment at the moment of failure.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<p>Starting a debug session. <code>--inspect-brk</code> is what you want for startup bugs because it waits for you before running a line:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-bash"># Attach at will; code runs immediately.\nnode --inspect app.js\n\n# Pause on line 1 and wait for a debugger to attach (debug startup code).\nnode --inspect-brk app.js\n\n# Open chrome://inspect in Chrome, click \"inspect\", or in VS Code use a\n# launch config. Then set breakpoints in the DevTools Sources panel.\n\n# Trigger the inspector on an ALREADY-running process (didn't use --inspect):\nkill -USR1 $(pgrep -f 'node app.js')</code></pre>`,
        `<p>The <code>debugger;</code> statement plus conditional logic — pause only on the interesting case instead of drowning in output:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">function processOrder(order) {\n  // Pauses here ONLY when the Inspector is attached AND this branch runs.\n  if (order.total < 0) {\n    debugger; // inspect 'order' live: full object, call stack, scope\n  }\n  return charge(order);\n}\n\n// Prefer a conditional breakpoint in DevTools (right-click the gutter) over\n// editing source: e.g. break on line 42 only when order.id === 'abc'.</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Capturing a diagnostic report programmatically — a portable, dependency-free post-mortem artifact you can attach to a bug ticket:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">import process from 'node:process';\n\n// Configure at startup: auto-write a JSON report on a fatal error or signal.\nprocess.report.reportOnFatalError = true;\nprocess.report.reportOnSignal = true;\nprocess.report.directory = './reports';\n\n// Or capture one on demand when something looks wrong:\nfunction snapshotOnAnomaly(reason) {\n  const file = process.report.writeReport();\n  console.error('wrote diagnostic report:', file, 'reason:', reason);\n  // The report contains: JS + native stack, heap/resource usage, libuv\n  // handles (open sockets/timers), env vars, and Node/OS versions.\n}</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Finding a memory leak by comparing heap growth. The code below deliberately leaks so the technique is concrete:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">const cache = []; // module-level: never garbage-collected\n\napp.get('/leak', (req, res) => {\n  // ❌ Grows forever — nothing ever removes entries. Classic leak.\n  cache.push(Buffer.alloc(1024 * 100, 'x'));\n  res.end('ok');\n});\n\n// Diagnose: run with --inspect, open DevTools > Memory. Take a heap snapshot,\n// hit /leak many times, take a SECOND snapshot, then use the \"Comparison\"\n// view. The retained Buffers under 'cache' light up as the delta. The fix is\n// an eviction policy (LRU, TTL) or not caching at module scope at all.\n\n// Quick numeric signal without DevTools:\nsetInterval(() => {\n  const { heapUsed } = process.memoryUsage();\n  console.log('heapUsed MB:', (heapUsed / 1048576).toFixed(1));\n}, 5000).unref();</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Exposing the inspector port in production.</strong> Port 9229 is a full remote-code-execution channel — anyone who can reach it can run arbitrary code in your process. Bind to localhost and tunnel over SSH; never <code>--inspect=0.0.0.0</code> on a public host.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Using <code>--inspect</code> when you need <code>--inspect-brk</code>.</strong> For startup or crash-on-boot bugs, plain <code>--inspect</code> lets the code run before you attach, so you miss the moment.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Shipping <code>debugger;</code> statements.</strong> With an inspector attached in some environments they halt the process; at best they're dead code. Lint them out of production builds.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Reaching for <code>console.log</code> on async races.</strong> Logging changes timing and can mask or move the race; a breakpoint that pauses everything is often clearer.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Debugging a leak with one heap snapshot.</strong> A single snapshot shows what's live, not what's <em>growing</em>. You need two (or three) snapshots and the comparison view to see the delta.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting to enable core dumps</strong> (<code>ulimit -c unlimited</code>) before a crash you want to post-mortem — after the crash it's too late.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Confusing a CPU-bound hang with a leak.</strong> A blocked event loop and a growing heap are different problems needing different profilers; check <code>memoryUsage()</code> and event-loop lag before assuming.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Assuming async stack traces are complete.</strong> They're much better in modern V8 but can still be truncated across certain boundaries; the <code>--stack-trace-limit</code> flag and <code>Error.stackTraceLimit</code> control depth.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Learn one real debugger well</strong> — VS Code's built-in Node debugger or Chrome DevTools — and make it your default over <code>console.log</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Use conditional and logpoint breakpoints</strong> so you pause only on the case that matters instead of stepping through thousands of iterations.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Keep the inspector bound to localhost</strong> and reach remote processes via an SSH tunnel; never expose 9229 publicly.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Enable diagnostic reports</strong> (<code>reportOnFatalError</code>/<code>reportOnSignal</code>) in production so crashes leave a portable artifact automatically.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Diagnose leaks with paired heap snapshots</strong> and the comparison view; look for objects retained by module-level state, caches, or un-removed listeners.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Profile CPU with the sampling profiler</strong> (DevTools Performance tab or <code>--cpu-prof</code>) before micro-optimising — measure where time actually goes.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Send <code>SIGUSR1</code> to attach to a live process</strong> you didn't start with <code>--inspect</code>, rather than restarting and losing the state.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Capture core dumps for unreproducible crashes</strong> and inspect them post-mortem with <code>llnode</code> or the diagnostic report.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How does <code>node --inspect</code> actually work?"</strong><br>A: The flag turns on V8's Inspector agent, which opens a WebSocket (default <code>127.0.0.1:9229</code>) speaking the Chrome DevTools Protocol. Any CDP client — Chrome's <code>chrome://inspect</code>, VS Code, or the terminal <code>node inspect</code> — attaches to that socket and exchanges JSON messages to set breakpoints, read scope, evaluate expressions, and take CPU/heap profiles. Because it's the same protocol Chrome uses for front-end JS, the familiar DevTools UI drives a Node backend unchanged.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What's the difference between <code>--inspect</code> and <code>--inspect-brk</code>?"</strong><br>A: Both open the inspector, but <code>--inspect-brk</code> pauses on the first line and waits for a debugger to attach before executing anything. Use it to debug startup code or a crash-on-boot — with plain <code>--inspect</code>, that code may run before you've connected, so you'd miss it.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q (trap): "Can you leave <code>--inspect</code> on in production so you can debug live?"</strong><br>A: You shouldn't. The inspector port is effectively a remote-code-execution backdoor — anyone who can reach 9229 can execute arbitrary code in your process. If you must debug live, bind to localhost only and reach it through an SSH tunnel, or send <code>SIGUSR1</code> to a running process to enable the inspector on demand, then disable it again. Never bind it to a public interface.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "A Node service's memory keeps climbing until it OOMs. How do you find the leak?"</strong><br>A: Attach with <code>--inspect</code> and open DevTools Memory. Take a heap snapshot, drive the workload that leaks, take a second snapshot, and use the comparison view to see which objects <em>grew</em> and, via the retainers, what's keeping them alive. Leaks are almost always still-reachable objects — a module-level array or cache that never evicts, a closure capturing large data, or an event listener that's never removed. One snapshot only shows what's live; the delta between two shows what's leaking.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What is a core dump and when would you use one?"</strong><br>A: A core dump is a full snapshot of a process's memory written when it crashes (enabled with <code>ulimit -c unlimited</code>). You load it into a post-mortem debugger like <code>llnode</code> to inspect the stack and heap of a process that's already dead. It's the tool for crashes you can't reproduce interactively — production-only or intermittent failures. Node's diagnostic report is a lighter JSON alternative capturing the stack, resource usage, and libuv handles at the moment of failure.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "The event loop seems blocked and requests hang, but memory is flat. What do you do?"</strong><br>A: That's a CPU/blocking problem, not a leak, so I reach for the CPU profiler (DevTools Performance tab or <code>--cpu-prof</code>) rather than heap snapshots. I'd look for a synchronous hot path — a big JSON parse, a sync crypto or fs call, a regex catastrophe, or a tight loop — that's monopolising the single thread. Measuring event-loop lag confirms the diagnosis, and the fix is usually to offload the work to a worker thread or make it async.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong><code>node --inspect</code></strong> → V8 Inspector WebSocket on <code>127.0.0.1:9229</code>, speaks Chrome DevTools Protocol.</li>`,
        `<li><strong><code>--inspect-brk</code></strong> pauses on line 1 for startup/crash-on-boot bugs.</li>`,
        `<li><strong><code>debugger;</code></strong> = source breakpoint when a debugger is attached; no-op otherwise.</li>`,
        `<li><strong><code>kill -USR1 &lt;pid&gt;</code></strong> enables the inspector on a running process.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Leak?</strong> Two heap snapshots + comparison view → find what grew &amp; its retainers.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Slow/blocked?</strong> CPU profiler (<code>--cpu-prof</code>) → find the synchronous hot path.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Crash you can't reproduce?</strong> Core dump (<code>ulimit -c unlimited</code>) or diagnostic report.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Security:</strong> never expose 9229 publicly — it's remote code execution.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },
    {
      title: 'Logging',
      slug: 'logging',
      order: 3,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>Logs are the primary way you understand a running system that you cannot attach a debugger to — which, in production, is almost always. When a customer reports "my payment failed at 2:14 pm," you can't step through their request; you can only search the record your system left behind. Good logs turn that into a two-minute lookup. Bad logs — unstructured, inconsistent, missing the request id, drowning in noise or leaking secrets — turn it into an afternoon of grep and guesswork, or a support ticket you simply cannot answer.</p>`,
        `<p>Logging is also a topic where junior and senior answers diverge sharply, so interviewers use it as a maturity signal. A junior says "use <code>console.log</code>." A senior talks about <em>structured</em> JSON logs, log <em>levels</em>, <em>correlation ids</em> that stitch one request across services, the performance cost of logging on the hot path, and — the one that really lands — a clear policy on what must <strong>never</strong> be logged: passwords, tokens, full card numbers, personal data. Getting that last part wrong is a security and compliance incident, not just a bug.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p><strong>Structured logging</strong> means emitting each log entry as machine-readable data — almost always a JSON object — instead of a free-form English sentence. Rather than <code>console.log('user 42 bought kit 7')</code>, you emit <code>{ level: 'info', msg: 'purchase', userId: 42, kitId: 7, requestId: 'abc' }</code>. The difference is enormous: a log platform (Datadog, Loki, CloudWatch, the ELK stack) can index those fields and let you query <code>userId=42 AND level=error</code> across billions of lines in seconds. Free-text logs can only be grepped, which doesn't scale and can't be aggregated into dashboards or alerts.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Log levels</strong> rank entries by severity — commonly <code>trace</code>, <code>debug</code>, <code>info</code>, <code>warn</code>, <code>error</code>, <code>fatal</code>. You set a threshold (usually <code>info</code> in production, <code>debug</code> in dev) and everything below it is suppressed, so you can dial verbosity without editing code. This is how you keep production logs affordable and readable while still being able to crank up detail when hunting a bug.</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch12-log-levels.svg|A pyramid of log levels from trace at the noisy base up to fatal at the severe top, with info as the production default|Log levels let one threshold control verbosity. Production usually sits at info; drop to debug/trace only while investigating.}}`,
        `<p>A <strong>correlation id</strong> (a.k.a. request id or trace id) is a unique token attached to every log line produced while handling a single request, propagated across every service the request touches. It's what lets you reconstruct the full story of one user action from thousands of interleaved lines emitted by many concurrent requests and processes.</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch12-correlation-id.svg|A request carrying one id through gateway, auth and orders services, all logging that id so a log aggregator can filter one request end to end|A correlation id threads one request across services. Filtering the aggregator by that id reconstructs the entire journey in order.}}`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p>In Node the two dominant structured loggers are <strong>pino</strong> and <strong>winston</strong>. Pino is built for speed: it serialises to JSON with minimal overhead and, crucially, offloads the expensive work — formatting, pretty-printing, shipping to a destination — to a separate <em>transport</em> process so the main thread isn't blocked writing logs. This matters because logging sits on your request hot path; a slow synchronous logger can throttle throughput. Winston is more feature-rich and flexible (many transports, custom formats) at some performance cost. Both emit JSON, both support levels, and both let you attach structured fields.</p>`,
        `<p>The mechanism that makes correlation ids ergonomic is the <strong>child logger</strong> plus <strong><code>AsyncLocalStorage</code></strong>. At the start of each request you read or mint a request id (typically from an incoming <code>x-request-id</code> header, or generate a UUID), then create a child logger bound to that id: <code>req.log = log.child({ requestId })</code>. Every line that child emits automatically includes the id — you never pass it manually. <code>AsyncLocalStorage</code> (from <code>node:async_hooks</code>) goes further: it keeps a per-request context alive across every <code>await</code> and callback in that request's async chain, so even code deep in a service layer, with no access to <code>req</code>, can grab the current request id from the ambient store. That's the modern replacement for threading a context object through every function signature.</p>`,
        `<p>Levels work by numeric comparison: each level has a value (pino uses <code>trace</code>=10 … <code>fatal</code>=60), the logger has a threshold, and a call is dropped cheaply if its level is below the threshold. Because the check happens before serialisation, disabled log calls are nearly free — but only if you don't do expensive work to <em>build</em> the log object before calling. Passing an object and letting the logger serialise lazily is faster than pre-formatting a string.</p>`,
        `<p>Finally, logs need somewhere to go. The <strong>twelve-factor</strong> guidance — which almost all container/cloud deploys follow — is that your app should just write logs to <strong>stdout/stderr</strong> as a stream and let the platform (Docker, Kubernetes, systemd) capture and route them to an aggregator. The app shouldn't manage log files, rotation, or shipping itself; that's the environment's job. This keeps the app stateless and portable.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<p>A pino logger with a per-request child bound to a correlation id — the everyday backbone of production logging:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">import pino from 'pino';\nimport { randomUUID } from 'node:crypto';\n\nconst log = pino({\n  level: process.env.LOG_LEVEL ?? 'info',\n  // Redact secrets so they never reach disk (see below).\n  redact: ['req.headers.authorization', '*.password', '*.token'],\n});\n\napp.use((req, res, next) => {\n  const requestId = req.headers['x-request-id'] ?? randomUUID();\n  req.log = log.child({ requestId, method: req.method, path: req.path });\n  res.setHeader('x-request-id', requestId); // echo it back to the client\n  req.log.info('request started');\n  res.on('finish', () => req.log.info({ status: res.statusCode }, 'request done'));\n  next();\n});\n\n// Deep in a handler — every line carries requestId automatically:\n// req.log.warn({ retries: 2 }, 'payment gateway slow');</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Propagating request context with <code>AsyncLocalStorage</code> so code that never sees <code>req</code> can still log the correlation id:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">import { AsyncLocalStorage } from 'node:async_hooks';\n\nconst als = new AsyncLocalStorage();\n\napp.use((req, res, next) => {\n  const requestId = req.headers['x-request-id'] ?? randomUUID();\n  // Everything downstream in this request's async chain shares this store.\n  als.run({ requestId }, () => next());\n});\n\n// A logger that auto-injects the ambient requestId — no plumbing needed.\nfunction ctxLog(level, obj, msg) {\n  const store = als.getStore();\n  log[level]({ ...obj, requestId: store?.requestId }, msg);\n}\n\n// Called from any service layer, across awaits, with zero context passed in:\nasync function chargeCard(amount) {\n  ctxLog('info', { amount }, 'charging card'); // still tagged with requestId\n}</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Using levels meaningfully and logging an error <em>object</em> so the stack is preserved as a structured field, not stringified:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">req.log.debug({ query }, 'running db query');      // dev detail, off in prod\nreq.log.info({ userId }, 'user logged in');          // normal milestone\nreq.log.warn({ latencyMs: 1400 }, 'slow upstream');  // suspicious, recovered\n\ntry {\n  await charge(order);\n} catch (err) {\n  // Pass the Error under a key the logger serialises specially (err/stack).\n  req.log.error({ err, orderId: order.id }, 'charge failed');\n  // ❌ Don't do: req.log.error('charge failed: ' + err) — loses the stack\n  //    and turns structured data into an un-queryable string.\n}</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Redaction — the difference between a log and a data breach. Never let secrets or PII reach the log sink:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">const log = pino({\n  redact: {\n    paths: [\n      'password', '*.password',\n      'req.headers.authorization',\n      'req.headers.cookie',\n      'creditCard', 'ssn', 'user.email',\n    ],\n    censor: '[REDACTED]',\n  },\n});\n\n// Even if a handler naively logs the whole request body, the sensitive\n// fields are replaced before serialisation:\nlog.info({ password: 'hunter2', user: { email: 'a@b.com' } }, 'signup');\n// => {\"level\":30,...,\"password\":\"[REDACTED]\",\"user\":{\"email\":\"[REDACTED]\"}}</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Logging secrets or PII.</strong> Passwords, tokens, session cookies, full card numbers, emails, and other personal data must never hit the logs — it's a security and compliance breach. Redact at the logger level so a careless log call can't leak them.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Unstructured string logs.</strong> <code>log.info('user ' + id + ' did ' + action)</code> can't be queried by field. Emit objects: <code>log.info({ userId: id, action }, 'user action')</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Stringifying errors.</strong> <code>log.error('failed: ' + err)</code> throws away the stack. Pass the Error object under an <code>err</code> key so the logger serialises name, message, and stack.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>One level for everything.</strong> Logging every detail at <code>info</code> makes production unreadable and expensive; logging nothing until <code>error</code> leaves you blind. Use the whole ladder deliberately.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Logging on the hot path synchronously.</strong> A slow, blocking logger throttles request throughput. Use an async/transport-based logger like pino and avoid pretty-printing in production.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Writing to files inside the app.</strong> Managing log files and rotation couples the app to its host. Write to stdout and let the platform handle capture, rotation, and shipping.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>No correlation id.</strong> Without a request id threaded through, you can't separate one user's request from thousands of interleaved lines across services.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Over-logging (noise).</strong> Logging inside tight loops or on every trivial branch buries the signal and inflates cost. Log decisions and boundaries, not every line executed.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Log structured JSON</strong> with a fast logger (pino) so entries are queryable and aggregatable, not just greppable.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Use log levels deliberately:</strong> <code>info</code> for milestones, <code>warn</code> for recoverable oddities, <code>error</code> for failed operations, <code>fatal</code> before the process dies; keep prod at <code>info</code> and lower the threshold to debug only while investigating.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Attach a correlation id to every request</strong> via a child logger, and propagate it across services through headers and <code>AsyncLocalStorage</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Log Error objects, not strings,</strong> so stack traces survive as structured fields.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Redact secrets and PII at the logger level</strong> with an explicit allow/deny policy — never rely on individual call sites to remember.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Write to stdout/stderr</strong> and let the platform route logs (twelve-factor); keep the app stateless.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Include useful context</strong> on each line — user id, route, status, duration — but log <em>events</em> and boundaries, not every statement.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Standardise a shared logger config</strong> across services so fields, levels, and redaction are consistent and dashboards work everywhere.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What is structured logging and why prefer it over <code>console.log</code>?"</strong><br>A: Structured logging emits each entry as machine-readable data — a JSON object with named fields — instead of a free-form sentence. That lets a log platform index and query by field (<code>userId=42 AND level=error</code>) across billions of lines, build dashboards, and fire alerts. Free-text logs can only be grepped, which doesn't aggregate or scale. <code>console.log</code> is unstructured, has no levels, and writes synchronously, so it's fine for a script but wrong for a service.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What is a correlation id and how do you propagate it in Node?"</strong><br>A: It's a unique token attached to every log line for one request and carried across every service the request touches, so you can reconstruct that request's full journey from interleaved logs. In Node you read or mint it (often from an <code>x-request-id</code> header), bind a child logger to it, and pass it downstream via request headers between services and via <code>AsyncLocalStorage</code> within a service — so even deep code with no access to the request object can log the current id without threading a context parameter through every function.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q (trap): "Should you log the request body so you can debug issues?"</strong><br>A: Not blindly. Request bodies routinely contain passwords, tokens, card numbers, and personal data, and logging them is a security and compliance breach. You configure redaction at the logger so sensitive fields are censored before serialisation, and you log only the specific, safe fields you actually need. The default posture is deny — never log a whole body or headers verbatim.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why is pino considered fast, and why does logger speed matter?"</strong><br>A: Logging sits on the request hot path, and Node is single-threaded, so a slow synchronous logger directly caps throughput. Pino minimises work on the main thread — it serialises straight to JSON and offloads formatting, pretty-printing, and shipping to a separate transport process. It also checks the level before doing any serialisation, so disabled log calls are nearly free. Winston is more flexible but does more work inline, which costs latency under load.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How should you log an error — what's the difference between passing the Error and the message?"</strong><br>A: Pass the Error object as a field (e.g. <code>log.error({ err }, 'charge failed')</code>). The logger has a special serializer that captures <code>name</code>, <code>message</code>, and the full <code>stack</code> as structured data you can later search and read. Concatenating the error into a string (<code>'failed: ' + err</code>) collapses it to <code>[object Error]</code> or a message with no stack, destroying the most useful debugging information and making the line un-queryable.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Where should a containerised Node app send its logs?"</strong><br>A: To stdout/stderr as a stream, per the twelve-factor app methodology. The application shouldn't manage log files, rotation, or shipping — that couples it to its host and adds state. The platform (Docker, Kubernetes, systemd) captures the streams and routes them to an aggregator like Loki, CloudWatch, or the ELK stack. This keeps the app stateless, portable, and easy to scale horizontally.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Structured JSON logs</strong> → queryable by field; beats greppable free text.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Levels:</strong> trace/debug (dev) · info (prod default) · warn · error · fatal.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>pino</strong> = fast, transport-offloaded, prod default; <strong>winston</strong> = flexible, heavier.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Correlation id:</strong> child logger + <code>x-request-id</code> header + <code>AsyncLocalStorage</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Log Error objects</strong> under an <code>err</code> key — keep the stack.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Never log</strong> passwords, tokens, cookies, card numbers, PII — redact at the logger.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Write to stdout</strong>; let the platform ship &amp; rotate (twelve-factor).</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },
  ],
};
